37
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:36,000
But I wanna just share a little bit about the climate work

38
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,190
that we're doing before I turn it over.

39
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:40,953
So, under Vision 2030,

40
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:45,060
the 116 California Community Colleges

41
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:47,820
have been asked to lead the way in climate action.

42
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:50,940
And so trying to take a holistic approach to that,

43
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:53,910
looking at opportunities for community engagement,

44
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:57,300
workforce development, facilities and operations,

45
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:01,410
so minimizing our greenhouse gas emissions from our campuses

46
00:02:01,410 --> 00:02:05,700
and also integrating climate action into the curriculum.

47
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:06,990
So the two speakers

48
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:08,850
who we're gonna hear from today

49
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:10,650
are gonna talk with us

50
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:13,170
about kind of two sides of that,

51
00:02:13,170 --> 00:02:15,780
the climate AI paradox,

52
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:18,210
so both potential solutions

53
00:02:18,210 --> 00:02:20,850
that AI can bring to climate challenges

54
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:22,050
and also some

55
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:26,310
of the energy consumption issues that we're dealing with.

56
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:28,290
And I think the speaker this morning

57
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:30,240
really set us up nicely for that.

58
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:33,180
So they're gonna share a little bit about their work,

59
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:35,130
and then I've got some questions for them,

60
00:02:35,130 --> 00:02:37,440
but I also want to make sure that we save time

61
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,120
for audience questions.

62
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:41,070
So please, as they're talking,

63
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:42,660
think about what questions you have,

64
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:45,780
because I will give you all an opportunity

65
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:48,090
to ask questions as we go.

66
00:02:48,090 --> 00:02:50,100
So without further ado,

67
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:53,130
I will start with Juliet.

68
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:54,900
So Juliet is a product director

69
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:58,920
of Earth & Resilience AI at Google,

70
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:00,930
where her teams lead over 20 projects

71
00:03:00,930 --> 00:03:04,350
focused on applying AI to enable a more resilient earth.

72
00:03:04,350 --> 00:03:06,330
Her team's work was the sole basis

73
00:03:06,330 --> 00:03:08,760
for Google being awarded Fast Company's

74
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:12,570
World Changing Company of the Year in 2024.

75
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:15,090
Juliet has focused on energy, transportation,

76
00:03:15,090 --> 00:03:17,310
and resilience for the past 20 years

77
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:21,030
and has worked in AI at Alphabet for the past eight.

78
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:24,390
Juliet previously led the enterprise PM team at DeepMind

79
00:03:24,390 --> 00:03:27,390
across industrial, efficiency, renewables,

80
00:03:27,390 --> 00:03:29,520
language, and document AI.

81
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,380
Before DeepMind, Juliet had

82
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:34,110
the in-car and app experiences at Waymo,

83
00:03:34,110 --> 00:03:35,640
and she led the cross-company launch

84
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:39,000
of Waymo's user-facing service.

85
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,300
So I am going to start off with Juliet

86
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:43,833
and ask her,

87
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:50,160
please share with us, in your perspective,

88
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:52,410
AI's potential for solving

89
00:03:52,410 --> 00:03:54,120
some of these environmental issues

90
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:56,523
and helping to save our planet.

91
00:03:57,540 --> 00:03:59,130
- Thank you very much, Carla,

92
00:03:59,130 --> 00:04:00,480
and thank you everyone for being here.

93
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,543
It's an honor for me to be here at a personal level as well.

94
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:06,240
My mom has spent 20 years working

95
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:07,710
in the community college industry,

96
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:10,380
and it's something that means a ton to me

97
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:11,820
to be able to give back in this way.

98
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,980
So, sorry, sometimes I get sentimental

99
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:15,270
when I think about where I've come from

100
00:04:15,270 --> 00:04:16,743
and where I get to be now.

101
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:20,310
But let's talk about what we get to do, right?

102
00:04:20,310 --> 00:04:23,403
And what can we do with AI and solutions in this space?

103
00:04:24,690 --> 00:04:27,480
We're lucky to have a whole variety of different areas.

104
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:29,820
And Carla, when you asked me to speak initially,

105
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:32,910
I thought I can give an overview of a number of them

106
00:04:32,910 --> 00:04:34,560
and then we can dig into any

107
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:36,180
that anyone has questions around.

108
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:38,400
But I just wanna give a flavor of the type of problems

109
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:40,170
that AI can solve.

110
00:04:40,170 --> 00:04:42,210
So we'll start out with talking about solutions

111
00:04:42,210 --> 00:04:45,240
in transportation and energy.

112
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,110
How many of you like stop-and-go traffic?

113
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:51,060
Nobody, right? (laughs)

114
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:52,410
Turns out the climate doesn't like

115
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:54,150
stop-and-go traffic either.

116
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:56,910
Pollution at intersections is 29 times higher

117
00:04:56,910 --> 00:04:58,440
than on open roads,

118
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:00,870
and over half of that comes from vehicles

119
00:05:00,870 --> 00:05:02,970
accelerating after they stop.

120
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:05,340
And so what our Project Greenlight team has been doing

121
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:09,030
is using Google Maps driving trends together with AI

122
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:12,210
to help traffic engineers make small changes

123
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:13,050
to traffic lights,

124
00:05:13,050 --> 00:05:16,177
think, take a few seconds off of the quote unquote,

125
00:05:16,177 --> 00:05:18,540
"green time" from this direction

126
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:21,030
and give a few more seconds to this direction,

127
00:05:21,030 --> 00:05:23,370
or coordinate this one traffic light

128
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:24,690
with another traffic light.

129
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:28,200
And what we've seen is that traffic engineers

130
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:31,140
are able to change an intersection in a matter of minutes,

131
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:33,390
and we've seen up to 30% reduction

132
00:05:33,390 --> 00:05:37,260
in stop-and-go traffic, up to 10% reduction in emissions.

133
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:39,930
We're now live in over 20 cities around the world,

134
00:05:39,930 --> 00:05:44,580
including Boston, my hometown of Seattle,

135
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:48,720
Rio de Janeiro, Jakarta, Indonesia, Bangalore,

136
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:50,100
a whole variety of cities

137
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:53,040
where we're seeing this capability generalize

138
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:54,810
and expand really quickly.

139
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:55,643
I've been to a number

140
00:05:55,643 --> 00:05:57,510
of different traffic engineering centers around the world.

141
00:05:57,510 --> 00:05:58,680
They're really fun to visit, by the way.

142
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:00,870
They have a lot of screens up everywhere.

143
00:06:00,870 --> 00:06:03,060
And traffic engineers try to get

144
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:05,400
to every traffic light in their city every five years

145
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:06,233
to retime it,

146
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:09,090
because their cities and their worlds are changing.

147
00:06:09,090 --> 00:06:10,560
You ask them whether they do that or not,

148
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:11,610
and they say, "No, of course I don't.

149
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:12,443
I don't have time.

150
00:06:12,443 --> 00:06:13,950
I can't get to all of these intersections."

151
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:17,820
And so AI is really able to help accelerate their ability

152
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:19,860
to get their work done to help us move

153
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:22,830
in a more sustainable way around cities.

154
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:25,290
And I think that this really shows the power of AI, right?

155
00:06:25,290 --> 00:06:26,550
I mean, I'm sure we've all noticed

156
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:29,520
just how quickly AI has shifted,

157
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:33,870
and with the beauty of AI's speed and scale

158
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:36,030
is that you can then get speed and scale

159
00:06:36,030 --> 00:06:38,520
across other industries and other infrastructure

160
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,677
where we need that for sustainability solutions.

161
00:06:40,677 --> 00:06:43,893
And so Greenlight is one example of that.

162
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:47,367
How many of you're familiar with airplane contrails?

163
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:48,450
Has anybody heard of this

164
00:06:48,450 --> 00:06:50,520
as a sustainability and a climate solution?

165
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:52,470
Yes, okay, that makes me very happy to hear,

166
00:06:52,470 --> 00:06:56,280
because previously, prior to our team working on this,

167
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:57,690
basically no one would've raised

168
00:06:57,690 --> 00:06:58,800
their hand in a room like this.

169
00:06:58,800 --> 00:06:59,633
And now we have a few,

170
00:06:59,633 --> 00:07:01,560
so I hope after this, all of you will be able

171
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:03,690
to raise your hand with what contrails are.

172
00:07:03,690 --> 00:07:06,780
But they're those little wispy clouds behind planes.

173
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:08,520
They're called a contrail.

174
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:10,500
And the thing that's interesting about contrails

175
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:12,120
is they're responsible for over 1/3

176
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:14,460
of aviation's warming impact.

177
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:17,130
Usually you think of fuel, right?

178
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:19,680
But contrails are actually really significantly warming.

179
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,210
They come from cirrus clouds.

180
00:07:21,210 --> 00:07:22,710
If any of you are outdoorsy

181
00:07:22,710 --> 00:07:24,030
and you like to camp,

182
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:28,680
you know that cloudy nights are warmer than cool nights,

183
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:29,513
or clear nights.

184
00:07:29,513 --> 00:07:31,560
The way that contrails work is very similar.

185
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:34,530
They trap heat into the Earth's atmosphere at night.

186
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:38,760
And we've used AI to predict where contrails will form

187
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:41,580
and had pilots in live flights

188
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:43,350
adjust the altitude of their flight

189
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:44,730
just like they do for turbulence,

190
00:07:44,730 --> 00:07:46,110
like you've probably seen,

191
00:07:46,110 --> 00:07:48,810
and they've reduced contrails by over 50%.

192
00:07:48,810 --> 00:07:50,250
We've done this with a number of flights

193
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:51,660
with American Airlines.

194
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:55,110
We're now working with EU air traffic control actually,

195
00:07:55,110 --> 00:07:57,300
where over a significant amount of airspace in Europe,

196
00:07:57,300 --> 00:07:59,340
they're actually automatically adjusting

197
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:00,630
the altitude of flights.

198
00:08:00,630 --> 00:08:02,400
And what's so exciting about this

199
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:05,640
is for those of you who follow sustainable aviation, right,

200
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:08,160
the other solutions in sustainability and aviation

201
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:10,200
look like electric planes,

202
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:12,690
hydrogen planes, biofuels.

203
00:08:12,690 --> 00:08:14,520
We need those solutions too.

204
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:17,190
But those solutions are gonna take decades to scale.

205
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:18,990
And the VP of sustainability

206
00:08:18,990 --> 00:08:21,630
for American Airlines went on national TV

207
00:08:21,630 --> 00:08:23,130
and said that this is something that can scale

208
00:08:23,130 --> 00:08:25,290
across the industry in two to five years.

209
00:08:25,290 --> 00:08:28,470
And so that's the power that you can bring of AI,

210
00:08:28,470 --> 00:08:31,170
is to really transform industries

211
00:08:31,170 --> 00:08:33,390
at a speed and scale that we have not seen

212
00:08:33,390 --> 00:08:36,270
through other solutions to date.

213
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:38,130
And we desperately need this speed, right?

214
00:08:38,130 --> 00:08:39,780
I think none of us in this room probably need

215
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:42,180
to be convinced that we have to act very quickly

216
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:45,390
from a sustainability standpoint.

217
00:08:45,390 --> 00:08:47,100
Oh, this is just a picture of how it works.

218
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:48,510
These are contrails on the left,

219
00:08:48,510 --> 00:08:50,490
you can see contrail from the ground

220
00:08:50,490 --> 00:08:51,323
in a photo.

221
00:08:51,323 --> 00:08:53,220
On the right, you can see them in satellite imagery.

222
00:08:53,220 --> 00:08:56,400
And so we trained an AI model to use satellite imagery

223
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:58,020
to be able to detect contrails.

224
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:00,600
And then we used AI as well,

225
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:02,610
this model coupled with weather data

226
00:09:02,610 --> 00:09:04,920
to predict where contrails will form.

227
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:07,740
And so that formed the basis of our work.

228
00:09:07,740 --> 00:09:11,070
I drove here today and was a few minutes late.

229
00:09:11,070 --> 00:09:12,120
Thank you for your patience.

230
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:14,130
Who else drove here today?

231
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:15,150
Yes?

232
00:09:15,150 --> 00:09:16,800
Who used Google Maps?

233
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:17,633
Yeah?

234
00:09:17,633 --> 00:09:19,020
Did you notice the green leaf

235
00:09:19,020 --> 00:09:21,090
in Google Maps when you were driving?

236
00:09:21,090 --> 00:09:23,520
Did you know before what it was?

237
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:24,930
Yeah, a couple dots. Great.

238
00:09:24,930 --> 00:09:25,770
This is a great room.

239
00:09:25,770 --> 00:09:28,950
So it's our fuel-efficient routing feature.

240
00:09:28,950 --> 00:09:31,380
So we share through Google Maps

241
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:32,760
the most fuel-efficient route

242
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:35,160
in cases where it isn't already the fastest,

243
00:09:35,160 --> 00:09:37,530
where the time is, you know, maybe just a little bit longer,

244
00:09:37,530 --> 00:09:38,400
30 seconds longer,

245
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:40,380
a couple of minutes longer, something like that.

246
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:41,850
And we've seen that this has been able

247
00:09:41,850 --> 00:09:43,680
to scale up really beautifully globally.

248
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:45,660
So we're now sharing this

249
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:47,820
with all Google Maps users around the world.

250
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:49,620
You can go into settings and you can turn it off

251
00:09:49,620 --> 00:09:53,160
if you like, but the majority of users really appreciate it.

252
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:56,920
And we see that we're able to save megatons of CO2

253
00:09:58,309 --> 00:10:00,360
through this work each year.

254
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:03,720
And so it's really able to drive a significant impact.

255
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:05,040
Another example, many of you

256
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:06,420
may have solar.

257
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:09,240
Solars are a great solution in California,

258
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:11,610
but if you have solar,

259
00:10:11,610 --> 00:10:13,290
I imagine many of you

260
00:10:13,290 --> 00:10:15,120
like the sustainability benefits of it,

261
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:17,850
but most people decide to go solar based on cost.

262
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:19,950
And so Google is an information company

263
00:10:19,950 --> 00:10:22,170
and we look to say, "Great, can we provide information

264
00:10:22,170 --> 00:10:24,240
so that people can make a well-rounded decision?"

265
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:26,310
And we've used AI models

266
00:10:26,310 --> 00:10:29,070
to help model out what the cost savings

267
00:10:29,070 --> 00:10:30,690
can be for hundreds of millions

268
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:33,660
of buildings worldwide if they go solar.

269
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:35,610
And what we've seen

270
00:10:35,610 --> 00:10:39,060
is that this significantly reduces the time

271
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:41,370
for a someone who's selling solar

272
00:10:41,370 --> 00:10:44,340
to convert someone into a purchaser.

273
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:46,740
And it reduces their cost of customer acquisition.

274
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:49,710
So we can actually make it cheaper for people to go solar

275
00:10:49,710 --> 00:10:52,470
by providing better information in this way.

276
00:10:52,470 --> 00:10:54,300
This is used by a very large percentage

277
00:10:54,300 --> 00:10:56,220
of solar installers in the U.S.

278
00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:58,020
and increasingly around the world

279
00:10:58,020 --> 00:11:00,573
to help build more sustainable solutions.

280
00:11:01,890 --> 00:11:05,940
So, that's kind of on the sustainability side.

281
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:07,590
Let's talk a bit more about resilience,

282
00:11:07,590 --> 00:11:11,070
because unfortunately, I'm sure many of you

283
00:11:11,070 --> 00:11:14,760
have been affected by the climate events

284
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:16,080
that we're already seeing.

285
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:18,300
If you lived in the Bay Area in 2020,

286
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:19,680
I'm sure you remember when the skies

287
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,420
were red from wildfires,

288
00:11:21,420 --> 00:11:24,600
and we're seeing an increasing number of disasters strike.

289
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:27,180
This has actually been a pretty good year, surprisingly,

290
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:29,310
but of course, you know, we had

291
00:11:29,310 --> 00:11:30,840
the flash floods in Texas, right?

292
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:33,120
And we had the fires in LA.

293
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:33,953
Even with that,

294
00:11:33,953 --> 00:11:35,850
it's actually been a better than average year.

295
00:11:35,850 --> 00:11:37,710
My team manages the crisis events

296
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:39,270
that get sent out to Google users.

297
00:11:39,270 --> 00:11:41,190
We send out millions of those every day.

298
00:11:41,190 --> 00:11:44,940
And so we monitor around the world disasters

299
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,590
and are able to help provide information

300
00:11:46,590 --> 00:11:48,330
to people who are in harm's way.

301
00:11:48,330 --> 00:11:50,250
And we use AI models

302
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:52,833
as a key part of providing this information.

303
00:11:54,198 --> 00:11:55,860
And part of this is because people turn to Google

304
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:57,390
in times of crisis, right?

305
00:11:57,390 --> 00:12:00,360
If there's a disaster, what should I do?

306
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:02,820
Let me check the internet, let me see what's going on.

307
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:04,170
And so this has been a natural role

308
00:12:04,170 --> 00:12:05,643
that Google has been playing.

309
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:07,560
One of the things that we do,

310
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:09,390
wildfires obviously strike close to home,

311
00:12:09,390 --> 00:12:11,607
I was a half mile away from the CCU wildfires

312
00:12:11,607 --> 00:12:13,323
and so we were evacuated.

313
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:16,200
And wildfire boundaries are a feature

314
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:17,490
that we've launched since,

315
00:12:17,490 --> 00:12:19,320
which have been extraordinarily helpful,

316
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,450
live in 27 countries

317
00:12:21,450 --> 00:12:24,300
where we show wildfire boundaries straight on Google Maps.

318
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:25,470
So in addition to being able

319
00:12:25,470 --> 00:12:27,090
to get your great boundaries from Watch Duty,

320
00:12:27,090 --> 00:12:29,460
which is a google.org grantee, by the way,

321
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:31,560
we're very supportive of their work,

322
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:34,590
as well as being able to get them from Cal Fire maps.

323
00:12:34,590 --> 00:12:36,900
You can also see boundaries

324
00:12:36,900 --> 00:12:40,530
right on Google Maps using AI and satellite imagery.

325
00:12:40,530 --> 00:12:42,060
Now, one of the things you might have noticed,

326
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:44,850
and I noticed this very much when we were evacuated,

327
00:12:44,850 --> 00:12:46,680
is that current satellite data's

328
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:49,320
not very good when it comes to wildfires.

329
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:52,140
So I was getting updates, if the fire crossed the road,

330
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:53,640
our community would've burned.

331
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:55,890
Fortunately, it didn't.

332
00:12:55,890 --> 00:12:58,470
But we were checking satellite inventory constantly,

333
00:12:58,470 --> 00:13:01,470
trying to refresh, saying, "Has the fire crossed the road?

334
00:13:01,470 --> 00:13:02,730
Is our community safe?"

335
00:13:02,730 --> 00:13:05,580
And we got updates twice a day,

336
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,380
because that's about how often

337
00:13:07,380 --> 00:13:09,420
you can see with that level of granularity.

338
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:12,570
And so our team recently launched the first satellite

339
00:13:12,570 --> 00:13:14,220
and a constellation of satellites

340
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:16,620
designed to provide much better data

341
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:18,150
for these types of scenarios,

342
00:13:18,150 --> 00:13:20,670
being able to detect a swimming pool-sized wildfire

343
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:22,623
anywhere on Earth within 20 minutes,

344
00:13:23,610 --> 00:13:26,220
compared to two to three acres today.

345
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:29,670
So really, really changing up the game using AI

346
00:13:29,670 --> 00:13:31,890
to process this massive amount of imagery

347
00:13:31,890 --> 00:13:34,770
to be able to provide fast wildfire detection.

348
00:13:34,770 --> 00:13:37,680
We need 52 satellites for this constellation to succeed.

349
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:38,700
So far we have one,

350
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:40,230
so it's gonna be a journey,

351
00:13:40,230 --> 00:13:42,513
but every journey starts somewhere, right?

352
00:13:43,507 --> 00:13:45,120
And we're taking the first step on this

353
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:47,850
in partnership with a nonprofit, the Earth Fire Alliance.

354
00:13:47,850 --> 00:13:49,980
If you know people who would like to contribute

355
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:53,610
to making this mission possible, we absolutely welcome that.

356
00:13:53,610 --> 00:13:55,620
But it's something that we are very committed to

357
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:58,233
and really excited to drive more of.

358
00:13:59,790 --> 00:14:02,130
Floods are another disaster that, you know,

359
00:14:02,130 --> 00:14:03,600
less relevant for California,

360
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:04,950
but relevant of course

361
00:14:04,950 --> 00:14:07,140
for many other places around the world.

362
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:08,880
And we have a global team.

363
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:12,240
And actually, the work for this flood forecasting

364
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:14,310
started out of one of our teams in the Middle East.

365
00:14:14,310 --> 00:14:16,380
That's an AI research team.

366
00:14:16,380 --> 00:14:19,290
Floods are one of the most deadly disasters

367
00:14:19,290 --> 00:14:20,553
around the world,

368
00:14:21,810 --> 00:14:24,330
affecting a tremendous number of people every year.

369
00:14:24,330 --> 00:14:28,200
And the other piece that we saw is that flood forecasting

370
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:30,630
and good flood forecasts correlate with GDP.

371
00:14:30,630 --> 00:14:33,450
So basically, richer countries have better flood forecasts

372
00:14:33,450 --> 00:14:34,980
and poorer, more vulnerable countries

373
00:14:34,980 --> 00:14:36,240
have worse flood forecasts.

374
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:40,050
And our team said, "Can we use an AI model to be able

375
00:14:40,050 --> 00:14:42,870
to provide more globally equitable flood forecasts

376
00:14:42,870 --> 00:14:43,950
around the world

377
00:14:43,950 --> 00:14:47,340
so that we could really help with communities who need?"

378
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:48,690
And the answer was yes.

379
00:14:48,690 --> 00:14:51,090
We published this work in "Nature,"

380
00:14:51,090 --> 00:14:52,710
and we're able to provide

381
00:14:52,710 --> 00:14:55,950
European-quality flood forecasts for Africa.

382
00:14:55,950 --> 00:15:00,210
We also were able to extend the lead time of floods

383
00:15:00,210 --> 00:15:02,970
up to seven days in advance using AI.

384
00:15:02,970 --> 00:15:06,210
So instead of saying, "Hey, the flood is here now,"

385
00:15:06,210 --> 00:15:08,017
with that same level of accuracy, we could say,

386
00:15:08,017 --> 00:15:10,800
"Hey, the flood will be here in seven days,"

387
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,810
providing significantly time,

388
00:15:12,810 --> 00:15:14,760
for those of you who have lived in flood zones, you know,

389
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:17,670
sandbags for example are one key solution,

390
00:15:17,670 --> 00:15:19,710
or protecting livestock and animals

391
00:15:19,710 --> 00:15:21,720
or moving your goods onto the second floor.

392
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:23,010
There are a whole variety of solutions

393
00:15:23,010 --> 00:15:25,560
that people can take if they have days notice ahead.

394
00:15:25,560 --> 00:15:27,450
And so this is something that we provide.

395
00:15:27,450 --> 00:15:30,750
These forecasts are freely available via Google Flood Hub

396
00:15:30,750 --> 00:15:32,310
for over a hundred countries

397
00:15:32,310 --> 00:15:35,553
covering locations where over 700 million people live.

398
00:15:36,450 --> 00:15:38,130
So this has been something

399
00:15:38,130 --> 00:15:40,684
that's been a big commitment of our team,

400
00:15:40,684 --> 00:15:41,910
and it is really important

401
00:15:41,910 --> 00:15:44,643
to us from a global equity standpoint.

402
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:47,790
Our work in cyclones also has gotten

403
00:15:47,790 --> 00:15:50,100
significant press this summer,

404
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:52,890
and we've already started to see cyclones hit.

405
00:15:52,890 --> 00:15:54,100
We recently released

406
00:15:54,990 --> 00:15:57,900
together between Google Research and Google DeepMind

407
00:15:57,900 --> 00:16:00,840
a breakthrough, experimental cyclones models

408
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:02,310
that can predict both the track

409
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:05,880
and the intensity of cyclones up to 15 days in advance.

410
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:07,650
This is now being tested this season

411
00:16:07,650 --> 00:16:09,450
by the National Hurricane Center,

412
00:16:09,450 --> 00:16:12,150
as well as some of our partners in Japan

413
00:16:12,150 --> 00:16:14,640
for predicting hurricanes.

414
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:17,700
And this recently, sorry for the ugly slide here,

415
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:20,580
but I screenshotted this from one of the former leads

416
00:16:20,580 --> 00:16:23,700
of the National Hurricane Center for hurricane forecasting,

417
00:16:23,700 --> 00:16:26,190
and Hurricane Aaron at the time this came out

418
00:16:26,190 --> 00:16:28,500
was the strongest storm of the year so far.

419
00:16:28,500 --> 00:16:30,810
This is just from a few weeks ago.

420
00:16:30,810 --> 00:16:32,520
Lower is better on these graphs.

421
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:34,200
These graphs are showing error,

422
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:36,720
and the model that we came out with is the red line.

423
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:38,970
And so we're seeing more accurate predictions

424
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:43,500
for both track and intensity of hurricanes using AI,

425
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,240
really driving a significant breakthrough

426
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:49,023
in a way that has not been seen in the past.

427
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:54,000
One other solution here is extreme heat.

428
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,160
So we've been working with the city of Miami,

429
00:16:56,160 --> 00:16:57,120
the city of Phoenix,

