00:21:25,980 --> 00:21:27,180
So why is this happening?

538
00:21:27,180 --> 00:21:28,470
And I want to get to the consequence,

539
00:21:28,470 --> 00:21:30,420
Carla, that you mentioned, 'cause that's important.

540
00:21:30,420 --> 00:21:33,720
So the culprits are these things called GPUs,

541
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:35,100
graphic processing units.

542
00:21:35,100 --> 00:21:37,203
And they had sort of an unassuming birth, right?

543
00:21:37,203 --> 00:21:40,230
They were meant to accelerate video and graphics.

544
00:21:40,230 --> 00:21:41,940
So, you know, when I was growing up,

545
00:21:41,940 --> 00:21:44,430
the cool kids had a GPU in their PC

546
00:21:44,430 --> 00:21:46,580
and they would be able to play video games.

547
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:48,660
As it turns out,

548
00:21:48,660 --> 00:21:52,020
AI requires some very specialized math processing.

549
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:54,870
And the same math that was required to accelerate,

550
00:21:54,870 --> 00:21:56,940
you know, the shoot 'em up games in video games

551
00:21:56,940 --> 00:21:58,470
actually happened to be the same math

552
00:21:58,470 --> 00:22:00,851
that powers autonomous vehicles,

553
00:22:00,851 --> 00:22:02,970
Bitcoin mining, and AI.

554
00:22:02,970 --> 00:22:05,190
So NVIDIA is, like, the luckiest company on earth

555
00:22:05,190 --> 00:22:06,600
because (chuckles) they built this thing

556
00:22:06,600 --> 00:22:07,500
for video processing,

557
00:22:07,500 --> 00:22:08,513
and now all of a sudden they're in the middle

558
00:22:08,513 --> 00:22:10,233
of this AI revolution.

559
00:22:11,310 --> 00:22:13,470
GPUs were never designed,

560
00:22:13,470 --> 00:22:15,273
now, I'm gonna channel my inner Karen Howe,

561
00:22:15,273 --> 00:22:18,210
they were never designed to be energy efficient.

562
00:22:18,210 --> 00:22:20,160
Never. Because why?

563
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:22,380
Right, they lived in these unassuming data centers.

564
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:26,130
They lived in scientific research facilities

565
00:22:26,130 --> 00:22:30,300
that did scientific modeling.

566
00:22:30,300 --> 00:22:33,150
So they were contained and yes, they used a lot of power,

567
00:22:33,150 --> 00:22:35,460
but they just weren't that many of 'em

568
00:22:35,460 --> 00:22:38,010
relatively speaking for most of history.

569
00:22:38,010 --> 00:22:40,773
And so there was really no incentive to make them cool.

570
00:22:42,180 --> 00:22:44,970
So they burned hot and they burned hotter and hotter.

571
00:22:44,970 --> 00:22:47,100
And when AI came about,

572
00:22:47,100 --> 00:22:48,810
so this is, I'm talking about 10 years ago,

573
00:22:48,810 --> 00:22:52,260
before ChatGPT, GPUs were found,

574
00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:54,960
as I mentioned, to accelerate the math for machine learning,

575
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:56,400
what we used to call machine learning,

576
00:22:56,400 --> 00:22:58,020
and when Gen AI came out,

577
00:22:58,020 --> 00:23:00,240
not only did that spread like wildfire

578
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:02,040
across the world, as we know,

579
00:23:02,040 --> 00:23:06,030
but the math required for GPT

580
00:23:06,030 --> 00:23:08,070
was even greater than anything we've seen.

581
00:23:08,070 --> 00:23:11,730
So just for reference, a ChatGPT query,

582
00:23:11,730 --> 00:23:12,563
so when you go into ChatGPT

583
00:23:12,563 --> 00:23:13,860
and you put in a prompt,

584
00:23:13,860 --> 00:23:15,270
that is 10x the energy

585
00:23:15,270 --> 00:23:17,460
it takes for a Google search, okay?

586
00:23:17,460 --> 00:23:20,610
And now, you already know that you're getting more

587
00:23:20,610 --> 00:23:21,963
out of that than, you know,

588
00:23:21,963 --> 00:23:23,670
this is nothing against Google,

589
00:23:23,670 --> 00:23:26,760
but GPT search is a lot more information

590
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:28,890
that you're getting certainly than sometimes a set of links.

591
00:23:28,890 --> 00:23:32,700
Either way, that energy required to generate that text

592
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:34,920
that you see in front of you is that much greater.

593
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:37,140
So these GPUs are really the culprit here,

594
00:23:37,140 --> 00:23:38,850
and they're not getting any cooler.

595
00:23:38,850 --> 00:23:41,820
And if you have an industry that is just taking off

596
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:43,590
and everybody needs these GPUs,

597
00:23:43,590 --> 00:23:44,880
you can't say, "Well, time out.

598
00:23:44,880 --> 00:23:47,820
Stop. Everybody hold up.

599
00:23:47,820 --> 00:23:48,870
Let me make these cooler

600
00:23:48,870 --> 00:23:49,860
and then you'll get 'em next year."

601
00:23:49,860 --> 00:23:51,420
No, it's like, "I need 'em now."

602
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:53,850
And that's part of my job is to talk to customers,

603
00:23:53,850 --> 00:23:56,430
and every day we're talking about how many GPUs they need,

604
00:23:56,430 --> 00:23:57,660
and I'm painfully aware

605
00:23:57,660 --> 00:24:01,560
that what they need to build to run these GPUs

606
00:24:01,560 --> 00:24:03,810
and the energy they need is gargantuan.

607
00:24:03,810 --> 00:24:06,600
So let me share kind of where we're going.

608
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:07,587
I mentioned that these data centers

609
00:24:07,587 --> 00:24:08,910
are gonna take all this energy.

610
00:24:08,910 --> 00:24:10,010
Here's where we're at.

611
00:24:11,490 --> 00:24:12,630
It is predicted

612
00:24:12,630 --> 00:24:16,650
that we will need 56 gigawatts of energy

613
00:24:16,650 --> 00:24:19,383
by the end of this decade to power all of this AI.

614
00:24:20,490 --> 00:24:22,350
When you look at what is being built,

615
00:24:22,350 --> 00:24:25,740
so the extra energy that's coming onto the grid,

616
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:29,433
and you look at what is,

617
00:24:30,645 --> 00:24:32,730
if you look at what's already coming on the grid

618
00:24:32,730 --> 00:24:35,550
and the extra capacity that is in our grid,

619
00:24:35,550 --> 00:24:39,180
we are still gonna be short 36 gigawatts of power

620
00:24:39,180 --> 00:24:40,830
by the end of this decade.

621
00:24:40,830 --> 00:24:42,210
Now, when you hear, you know,

622
00:24:42,210 --> 00:24:44,250
this gigawatt, that gigawatt, 10 gigawatt,

623
00:24:44,250 --> 00:24:45,240
you might be like, you know,

624
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:46,687
Dr. Emmett Brown in "Back to the Future."

625
00:24:46,687 --> 00:24:48,720
"1.21 gigawatts, oh my God,

626
00:24:48,720 --> 00:24:50,220
how could I have been so stupid?"

627
00:24:50,220 --> 00:24:53,400
Okay, so 36-gigawatt shortfall

628
00:24:53,400 --> 00:24:55,590
is enough to power 1/5

629
00:24:55,590 --> 00:24:57,180
of the households in the United States,

630
00:24:57,180 --> 00:24:59,823
or all the homes in Texas and California combined.

631
00:25:01,290 --> 00:25:02,460
Recent studies have shown

632
00:25:02,460 --> 00:25:03,870
that that's off by a factor of two.

633
00:25:03,870 --> 00:25:07,530
We're actually (chuckles) gonna be short 70 gigawatts.

634
00:25:07,530 --> 00:25:08,910
So the consequence, Carla, sorry,

635
00:25:08,910 --> 00:25:09,960
finally getting to your question,

636
00:25:09,960 --> 00:25:12,600
the consequence is a few things.

637
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:15,180
One is that it puts a break on the whole system,

638
00:25:15,180 --> 00:25:16,740
and in fact I've talked to,

639
00:25:16,740 --> 00:25:17,940
I have the privilege of talking

640
00:25:17,940 --> 00:25:19,500
to a lot of different people about AI,

641
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:21,870
and I've learned an immense amount in the past few years.

642
00:25:21,870 --> 00:25:23,970
Power is a huge problem,

643
00:25:23,970 --> 00:25:26,340
and it actually is putting the brakes on projects.

644
00:25:26,340 --> 00:25:29,700
I've met some partners of ours that are literally,

645
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:32,490
they have equipment in their lab that they can't power up

646
00:25:32,490 --> 00:25:35,700
because the local utility has not been able to dig a trench

647
00:25:35,700 --> 00:25:39,000
to get power to their lab.

648
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:42,120
So it's putting the brakes on things, number one.

649
00:25:42,120 --> 00:25:45,510
Number two, which I think is interesting

650
00:25:45,510 --> 00:25:46,980
just from a technical point of view,

651
00:25:46,980 --> 00:25:49,800
is it's revived the whole concept of nuclear power.

652
00:25:49,800 --> 00:25:52,260
Now, we're immigrants to the United States,

653
00:25:52,260 --> 00:25:55,680
very proud immigrants, moved here in 1982 to New York,

654
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:58,500
and, you know, the memory of Three Mile Island

655
00:25:58,500 --> 00:25:59,820
was very fresh at that time.

656
00:25:59,820 --> 00:26:01,590
It wasn't anywhere near where we lived,

657
00:26:01,590 --> 00:26:02,850
but, like, people talked about it.

658
00:26:02,850 --> 00:26:05,190
Like, I remember in fifth grade, you know,

659
00:26:05,190 --> 00:26:06,577
my teacher talking about Three Mile Island,

660
00:26:06,577 --> 00:26:09,390
and I was just beside myself that they're restarting

661
00:26:09,390 --> 00:26:11,030
one of the reactors of Three Mile Island

662
00:26:11,030 --> 00:26:13,200
(chuckles) to power a Microsoft data center.

663
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:14,880
I was like, "What are we gonna spin up next? Chernobyl?

664
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:16,530
I mean, come on. Like, why stop there?"

665
00:26:16,530 --> 00:26:18,360
So, but in all seriousness,

666
00:26:18,360 --> 00:26:20,130
the nuclear power that they're thinking about today,

667
00:26:20,130 --> 00:26:21,330
I'm not here to advocate for it,

668
00:26:21,330 --> 00:26:23,190
I'm just talking about a solution

669
00:26:23,190 --> 00:26:25,620
that is potentially possible,

670
00:26:25,620 --> 00:26:27,720
are very small modular nuclear reactors.

671
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:30,480
They're about the size of the lot of your home,

672
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:34,380
and they run locally and they can power these data centers.

673
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:36,330
Interestingly enough, they actually have

674
00:26:36,330 --> 00:26:37,980
some bipartisan support,

675
00:26:37,980 --> 00:26:40,500
because as Karen Howe was talking about today,

676
00:26:40,500 --> 00:26:42,030
and it may be obvious

677
00:26:42,030 --> 00:26:44,160
from some of the reporting you've seen,

678
00:26:44,160 --> 00:26:46,590
what we don't want is a situation like Memphis,

679
00:26:46,590 --> 00:26:47,790
which is incredibly sad.

680
00:26:47,790 --> 00:26:48,960
I dunno if you've seen the reporting,

681
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:50,700
but basically Grok came in there

682
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:51,870
and there's 30,

683
00:26:51,870 --> 00:26:53,100
I mean, she said 35,

684
00:26:53,100 --> 00:26:55,050
I thought it was (chuckles) more.

685
00:26:55,050 --> 00:26:57,150
Ether way, these things are just spewing

686
00:26:57,150 --> 00:27:01,077
this just noxious gas into the community.

687
00:27:01,077 --> 00:27:02,407
(chuckles) And the city leaders are like,

688
00:27:02,407 --> 00:27:03,240
"Well, we didn't know."

689
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:04,830
So, you know, they were duped.

690
00:27:04,830 --> 00:27:07,470
We don't want that for every data center.

691
00:27:07,470 --> 00:27:09,420
So in a way, I think part of the reason

692
00:27:09,420 --> 00:27:11,670
these nuclear reactors, these small nuclear reactors,

693
00:27:11,670 --> 00:27:14,610
are getting all this attention and bipartisan support

694
00:27:14,610 --> 00:27:15,690
where they wouldn't is fundamentally,

695
00:27:15,690 --> 00:27:17,460
they're not Chernobyl, they're not Three Mile Island.

696
00:27:17,460 --> 00:27:18,960
It's a completely different world today.

697
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:20,700
You can design these reactors.

698
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:22,020
But it's a way to provide

699
00:27:22,020 --> 00:27:24,120
that 36-gigawatt shortfall

700
00:27:24,120 --> 00:27:27,330
without pumping noxious fumes into the air, right?

701
00:27:27,330 --> 00:27:28,770
There's very few technologies

702
00:27:28,770 --> 00:27:30,450
that can actually make up for the shortfall.

703
00:27:30,450 --> 00:27:33,030
So it's bringing into conversation

704
00:27:33,030 --> 00:27:35,220
technologies that we've forgotten about,

705
00:27:35,220 --> 00:27:36,120
but it's bringing it up

706
00:27:36,120 --> 00:27:40,800
because actually, a lot of people don't want a consequence

707
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:42,570
where we're generating this great AI

708
00:27:42,570 --> 00:27:44,280
but we're causing all this environmental harm.

709
00:27:44,280 --> 00:27:46,710
I mean, Juliet presented some amazing examples

710
00:27:46,710 --> 00:27:50,380
how AI can be used to make small changes, big changes

711
00:27:51,450 --> 00:27:53,220
to help the environment.

712
00:27:53,220 --> 00:27:54,780
Well, this is one way to do it, right?

713
00:27:54,780 --> 00:27:56,520
Nuclear power is actually able to provide

714
00:27:56,520 --> 00:27:58,050
that much energy to power

715
00:27:58,050 --> 00:28:00,570
these power-hungry GPUs

716
00:28:00,570 --> 00:28:02,970
without spewing noxious fumes into the air, right?

717
00:28:02,970 --> 00:28:06,480
There are other consequences, but this is one option.

718
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:08,610
But on a more philosophical level,

719
00:28:08,610 --> 00:28:12,210
now I'm speaking as Gaurav Khanna, not as a Cisco employee,

720
00:28:12,210 --> 00:28:16,950
what I think will happen is that people will pause, okay?

721
00:28:16,950 --> 00:28:19,140
If you cannot get enough power,

722
00:28:19,140 --> 00:28:20,850
'cause the utility cannot dig a trench

723
00:28:20,850 --> 00:28:23,550
to lay the power line down to power your AI,

724
00:28:23,550 --> 00:28:26,010
you have to stop and think, "What am I doing this for?

725
00:28:26,010 --> 00:28:28,260
What am I building? Do I need this much power?

726
00:28:28,260 --> 00:28:30,060
Do I need this much AI?"

727
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:31,050
And if you think about it,

728
00:28:31,050 --> 00:28:33,210
that's a very fundamentally different approach

729
00:28:33,210 --> 00:28:34,770
than we took when the internet launched

730
00:28:34,770 --> 00:28:36,660
and when the cloud computing became big,

731
00:28:36,660 --> 00:28:37,800
it's like, oh, great, internet,

732
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:39,570
boom, everybody, (chuckles) use the internet browser.

733
00:28:39,570 --> 00:28:43,410
You know, it boomed, the sale of PCs boomed

734
00:28:43,410 --> 00:28:44,760
in the wake of the internet,

735
00:28:44,760 --> 00:28:46,110
and cloud computing,

736
00:28:46,110 --> 00:28:49,530
the data centers and the data center footprint

737
00:28:49,530 --> 00:28:51,690
boomed without really any consequence

738
00:28:51,690 --> 00:28:52,530
in thinking about maybe,

739
00:28:52,530 --> 00:28:53,580
you know, what's this gonna mean?

740
00:28:53,580 --> 00:28:54,690
Like, people just built it out.

741
00:28:54,690 --> 00:28:56,700
We wanted more salesforce.com, we wanted more Google,

742
00:28:56,700 --> 00:28:58,890
we wanted more Meta.

743
00:28:58,890 --> 00:29:01,950
With AI, I feel very early on in the cycle,

744
00:29:01,950 --> 00:29:04,200
the fact that we're short on power

745
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:06,157
will make people pause and think,

746
00:29:06,157 --> 00:29:08,040
"What on earth are we doing here,

747
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:10,410
and how can we build this properly?"

748
00:29:10,410 --> 00:29:12,870
And Jory said something really great this morning

749
00:29:12,870 --> 00:29:16,290
about being intentional about equity

750
00:29:16,290 --> 00:29:19,590
in the process of thinking about education by design.

751
00:29:19,590 --> 00:29:22,470
I actually just saw him outside, by design.

752
00:29:22,470 --> 00:29:24,960
He said that and I told him I'd work it in.

753
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:27,390
So if you see Jory, tell him I worked it in,

754
00:29:27,390 --> 00:29:28,740
because you have to think

755
00:29:28,740 --> 00:29:30,120
about the environmental consequences

756
00:29:30,120 --> 00:29:32,130
by design from the beginning,

757
00:29:32,130 --> 00:29:33,510
not some afterthought later

758
00:29:33,510 --> 00:29:34,920
after, you know, half the things

759
00:29:34,920 --> 00:29:36,750
that you care about are destroyed.

760
00:29:36,750 --> 00:29:39,990
And so I think giving people a moment to pause and reflect

761
00:29:39,990 --> 00:29:43,470
and think about how we do this intentionally by design

762
00:29:43,470 --> 00:29:45,870
is probably gonna be one of the greatest legacies

763
00:29:45,870 --> 00:29:47,490
of this power shortfall.

764
00:29:47,490 --> 00:29:48,630
And I'm starting to see it now,

765
00:29:48,630 --> 00:29:50,250
because people are putting things on hold,

766
00:29:50,250 --> 00:29:51,330
and when you put things on hold,

767
00:29:51,330 --> 00:29:52,980
you have a time to stop and reflect.

768
00:29:52,980 --> 00:29:55,440
So I think those are the three consequences.

769
00:29:55,440 --> 00:29:56,273
Yeah.

770
00:30:00,990 --> 00:30:01,823
- Okay.

771
00:30:01,823 --> 00:30:06,090
I will pose the next question to Juliet.

